scription":"Test Your News I.Q. 2026 Elections Election Results Election calendar White House Congress Supreme Court The latest AP-NORC polls. 2026 Elections Election Results Election calendar White House Congress Supreme Court The latest AP-NORC polls. President Donald Trump used a primetime address to the nation Thursday to elevate his yearslong push to raise doubts about the legitimacy of U.S. elections and dispute his 2020 loss in an appeal for more restrictive voting laws ahead of the midterms. “Starting during the 2020 election cycle, the People’s Republic of China carried out what is believed to be the largest compromise of election data in history, resulting in China’s illicit acquisition of 220 million U.S. voter files,” Trump said Thursday night. In his remarks, Trump touted the release of a new Department of Homeland Security investigation, based on state voter rolls and public records, that he said had identified approximately 278,000 noncitizens registered to vote in federal elections."},{"title":"Newly declassified documents offer scant evidence for Trump’s election claims - NBC News","url":"https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/elections/newly-declassified-documents-scant-evidence-trump-election-claims-rcna587971","description":"IE 11 is not supported. For an optimal experience visit our site on another browser. # Newly declassified documents offer scant evidence for Trump’s election claims. The president attempted to paint a picture of a vulnerable election system, but the documents don’t fully back up his allegations. President Donald Trump delivered an address to the nation from the East Room of the White House on July 16, 2026.Saul Loeb / Pool / AFP via Getty Images. Add NBC News to Google. President Donald Trump’s sweeping claims of foreign interference in elections and the vulnerability of voting to attacks aren’t fully backed up by the newly declassified files released by his administration. We are getting your experience ready."}],"name":"web_search_tavily","tool_call_id":"toolu_0174NDVgajdfHMu65f5cBTrR"},{"role":"tool","content":[{"title":"Map: 7.3-Magnitude Earthquake Hits Off Mexico's Coast - The New York Times","url":"https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/07/17/world/earthquake-mexico-guatemala.html","description":"# Map: 7.3-Magnitude Earthquake Hits Off Mexico's Coast. Note: Map shows the area with a shake intensity of 3 or greater, which U.S.G.S. defines as “weak,” though the earthquake may be felt outside the areas shown. All times on the map are Mexico Central time. A major, 7.3-magnitude earthquake struck in the North Pacific Ocean on Friday, according to the United States Geological Survey. The temblor happened at 8:48 a.m. Mexico Central time about 30 miles southwest of Aquiles Serdán, Mexico, data from the agency shows. U.S.G.S. data earlier reported that the magnitude was 7.4. As seismologists review available data, they may revise the earthquake’s reported magnitude. Additional information collected about the earthquake may also prompt U.S.G.S. scientists to update the shake-severity map. ## Quakes and aftershocks within 100 miles. Shake data is as of Friday, July 17 at 9:08 a.m. Mexico Central time. Aftershocks data is as of Friday, July 17 at 10:17 a.m. Mexico Central time."},{"title":"Mexico Earthquake: Map Shows Where 7.3 Magnitude Tremor Hit Near Puerto Madero - Newsweek","url":"https://www.newsweek.com/mexico-earthquake-puerto-madero-usgs-map-magnitude-12211196","description":"# Mexico Earthquake: Map Shows Where 7.3 Magnitude Tremor Hit Coastline. A powerful magnitude 7.3 earthquake struck off the southern coast of Mexico early Friday morning, rattling the region and triggering immediate infrastructure reviews by emergency officials. The U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) said the quake struck near the Pacific coast of Chiapas state. The tremor occurred at a shallow depth, a factor that typically amplifies the intensity of shaking and expands the area where it can be felt. ## 7.3 Magnitude Earthquake in Mexico Today: Where Was the Epicenter? Preliminary data from the USGS placed the epicenter approximately 48 kilometers (30 miles) southwest of Aquiles Serdán, a coastal community in Chiapas, and not far from the port town of Puerto Madero near the Guatemala border. A map from the USGS shows where the powerful quake struck in southern Mexico on Friday. *Contact Newsweek editors for this story: Kate Nalepinski and Anthony Murray.*."},{"title":"Magnitude 7.3 quake hits Mexican coast near Guatemala, triggering tsunami alert","url":"https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20260717-magnitude-7-3-quake-hits-mexican-coast-near-guatemala-triggering-tsunami-alert","description":"*   By clicking \"Accept\", you consent to the use of cookies or similar technologies by France Médias Monde and its partners. A powerful earthquake struck the southern Mexican Pacific coast on Friday, prompting residents to evacuate buildings in neighbouring Guatemala and El Salvador – and triggering an alert from the US Tsunami Warning System. Image 2: People evacuate a building after a powerful 7.4-magnitude earthquake struck off the coast of Mexico's southern state of Chiapas, triggering a tsunami warning and shaking buildings in neighboring Guate. People evacuate a building in San Salvador on July 17, 2026, after a powerful 7.4-magnitude earthquake struck off the coast of Mexico's southern state of Chiapas, triggering a tsunami warning and shaking buildings in neighbouring Guatemala and El Salvador. A powerful magnitude 7.3earthquakestruck near the coast of Mexico's southernmost state of Chiapas on Friday, triggering a tsunami warning and shaking buildings in neighbouring Guatemala and El Salvador."},{"title":"7.3 magnitude earthquake strikes Mexico and Guatemala coasts ...","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2026/07/17/magnitude-73-earthquake-strikes-mexico-and-guatemala-coasts-tsunami-alert-issued","description":"In each episode, two political heavyweights from across the EU face off to propose a diversity of opinions and spark conversations around the most important issues of EU affairs and the wider European political life. ## A 7.3 magnitude earthquake shook the coasts of Mexico and Guatemala on Friday, its epicentre off Chiapas at about 15 km deep, triggering a tsunami alert for dangerous waves within a 300 km radius. A 7.3-magnitude earthquake struck the coast of the Mexican state of Chiapas on Friday, near the border with Guatemala, triggering a tsunami alert for several areas of the Pacific. The tremor was felt most strongly in the Mexican states of Chiapas and Oaxaca, as well as in Guatemala and El Salvador, according to journalists on the ground. In a post on X, Mexican President Claudia Sheinbaum said she had spoken with the governors of Chiapas and Tabasco and that, so far, no damage had been reported in either state."},{"title":"Major magnitude 7.3 earthquake reported off coast of Mexico in Pacific Ring of Fire | Fox Weather","url":"https://www.foxweather.com/extreme-weather/tsunami-threat-issued-mexico-guatemala-after-major-magnitude-7-3-earthquake","description":"The FOX Weather App Learn More. Fox Weather App on an iPhone, Fox Weather logo overlapping. # Tsunami threat issued for Mexico, Guatemala after magnitude 7.3 earthquake along the Pacific Ring of Fire. ## A tsunami threat was issued for Mexico and Guatemala within 186 miles of the earthquake's epicenter. ## Magnitude 7.3 earthquake strikes off the coast of western Mexico. A major earthquake struck off the coast of Mexico, near the Guatemala border on Friday morning, causing a tsunami threat to be issued for the coasts of both countries. The earthquake happened at 7:48 a.m. Pacific time, and was originally reported as a magnitude 7.4 before being recalculated as a magnitude 7.3. **UNDERSTANDING THE PACIFIC RING OF FIRE: WHERE EARTHQUAKES AND VOLCANOES MEET**. **HOW TO WATCH FOX WEATHER**. This is the latest in a string of large earthquakes in the Pacific Ring of Fire. The Ring of Fire is a 25,000-mile belt of volcanoes and earthquake zones surrounding much of the Pacific Ocean."}],"name":"web_search_tavily","tool_call_id":"toolu_014MVsoDUvx1xR5WczpqmHnu"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"toolCalls":[{"id":"toolu_01JTB3H1eYyWwTd7T5FnLaQk","name":"web_search_tavily","arguments":{"query":"Iran war news","count":6,"topic":"news","searchDepth":"advanced"}},{"id":"toolu_019xz9y8vDETqkHttp2mWvu5","name":"web_search_tavily","arguments":{"query":"breaking news","count":8,"topic":"news"}}]},{"role":"tool","content":[{"title":"U.S. deploys B-1 bomber as attacks on Iran intensify","url":"https://www.axios.com/2026/07/23/b-1-bomber-iran-war","description":"[Skip to main content](https://www.axios.com/2026/07/23/b-1-bomber-iran-war#main-content). *   [Newsletters](https://www.axios.com/newsletters). *   [Axios Pro](https://www.axios.com/pro/all-deals). *   [Axios Live](https://www.axios.com/events). *   [The Axios Show](https://www.axios.com/the-axios-show). [Axios](https://www.axios.com/). [](https://www.axios.com/results). [Axios](https://www.axios.com/). 8 hours ago - [World](https://www.axios.com/world). *   [Barak Ravid](https://www.axios.com/authors/barak_ravid). [Add Axios on Google](https://google.com/preferences/source?q=axios.com). [Add Axios on Google](https://google.com/preferences/source?q=axios.com). The U.S. military used a B-1 long-range bomber on Tuesday to strike Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps targets in [Iran](https://www.axios.com/world/iran), U.S. officials said. **Why it matters:** It was the first time the U.S. conducted a[B-1](https://www.axios.com/2026/01/03/us-aircraft-maduro-raid-caine-venezuela)mission since fighting with Iran resumed 12 days ago. *   Amid a continued U.S. military build up in the region, President [Trump](https://www.axios.com/politics-policy/donald-trump) is still [considering](https://www.axios.com/2026/07/21/iran-war-ceasefire-proposal-trump-troops) returning to major combat operations against Iran. *   On Wednesday, President Trump [threatened](https://www.axios.com/2026/07/22/trump-iran-bridges-power-plants-hormuz) to bomb bridges and power plants, including in Tehran, if Iran attacks more ships in the Strait of Hormuz. [Explore Axios Newsletters](https://www.axios.com/newsletters). *   [About Axios](https://www.axios.com/about). *   [Advertise with us](https://www.axios.com/advertise). *   [Careers](https://www.axios.com/careers). *   [Contact us](https://www.axios.com/contact). *   [Newsletters](https://www.axios.com/newsletters). *   [Axios Live](https://www.axios.com/events). *   [Axios HQ](https://www.axioshq.com/?utm_source=axios&utm_medium=site&utm_campaign=axioshq). *   [Privacy policy](https://www.axios.com/legal). *   [Terms of use](https://www.axios.com/legal/terms-of-use). [Axios Homepage](https://www.axios.com/). ![Image 9](https://t.co/1/i/adsct?bci=4&dv=UTC%26en-US%2Cen%26Google%20Inc.%26Linux%20x86_64%26255%261280%261280%268%2624%261280%261280%260%26na&eci=3&event=%7B%7D&event_id=bb609046-5f64-40ca-b4b9-cac4f3299a96&integration=gtm&p_id=Twitter&p_user_id=0&pl_id=8bb5bb11-03fe-4742-8554-f0002e6204ed&pt=U.S.%20deploys%20B-1%20bomber%20as%20attacks%20on%20Iran%20intensify&tw_document_href=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.axios.com%2F2026%2F07%2F23%2Fb-1-bomber-iran-war&tw_iframe_status=0&tw_pid_src=1&twpid=tw.1784803259257.742204083371098988&txn_id=o6ppz&type=javascript&version=2.3.54)![Image 10](https://analytics.twitter.com/1/i/adsct?bci=4&dv=UTC%26en-US%2Cen%26Google%20Inc.%26Linux%20x86_64%26255%261280%261280%268%2624%261280%261280%260%26na&eci=3&event=%7B%7D&event_id=bb609046-5f64-40ca-b4b9-cac4f3299a96&integration=gtm&p_id=Twitter&p_user_id=0&pl_id=8bb5bb11-03fe-4742-8554-f0002e6204ed&pt=U.S.%20deploys%20B-1%20bomber%20as%20attacks%20on%20Iran%20intensify&tw_document_href=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.axios.com%2F2026%2F07%2F23%2Fb-1-bomber-iran-war&tw_iframe_status=0&tw_pid_src=1&twpid=tw.1784803259257.742204083371098988&txn_id=o6ppz&type=javascript&version=2.3.54). ![Image 11](https://bat.bing.com/action/0?ti=97201771&tm=gtm002&Ver=2&mid=f726d1a4-08d2-42fd-a236-35483aa3c1a8&bo=1&sid=fe9d4e00868211f1bfd10fcb4cae2faa&vid=fea40340868211f1bcc9b13338205ca7&vids=1&msclkid=N&pi=918639831&lg=en-US&sw=1280&sh=1280&sc=24&tl=U.S.%20deploys%20B-1%20bomber%20as%20attacks%20on%20Iran%20intensify&kw=Military,Iran,Aircraft,Air%20Force,Airstrikes&p=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.axios.com%2F2026%2F07%2F23%2Fb-1-bomber-iran-war&r=&lt=1190&evt=pageLoad&sv=2&cdb=ARoR&rn=203473)."},{"title":"Trump's nuclear deal with Saudis jettisons longstanding US demands","url":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cz05lx0xpero","description":"The Trump administration's nuclear agreement with Saudi Arabia reversed a years-long stance toward a key Middle East ally. President Donald Trump gave Saudi Arabia what it wanted without extracting concessions for Israel in return - and over objections from critics who argued that it could spark a nuclear arms race in the region. The deal was the latest example of Trump jettisoning the traditional American approach to the Middle East, which centred on Israel and finding a two-state solution to the Israeli-Palestinian conflict. \"Countries in the Middle East want to develop their nuclear infrastructure and they're going to do it even if the US is not involved. But signing a nuclear pact in the Middle East is an especially bold move that will raise tensions in a volatile region where the US is at war with Iran. It represents a major victory for Saudi Arabia's Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, who has sought a nuclear agreement with the US for years as part of a broader campaign to strengthen relations with the West."},{"title":"Amish Shah wins Democratic primary for key Arizona House district, defeating candidate backed by national party groups - CBS News","url":"https://www.cbsnews.com/news/arizona-1st-district-democratic-primary-amish-shah","description":"# Amish Shah wins Democratic primary for key Arizona House district, defeating candidate backed by national party groups. Democrat Amish Shah will take on GOP nominee Jay Feely in the general election for Arizona's 1st Congressional District, The Associated Press projects, following primaries for both parties on Tuesday. Galán-Woods — a former broadcast journalist who was married to the late Grant Woods, a former Arizona attorney general who left the Republican Party over President Trump — had the backing of the national party in the primary against Shah, a physician and former state legislator who was the Democrats' nominee in 2024. Feely, an NFL veteran and former analyst for CBS Sports, initially ran in Arizona's 5th District, but Mr. Trump endorsed one of Feely's primary opponents and urged him to run in a different district or for another office. Mr. Trump then issued a double endorsement in the 1st District primary, backing Feely and Gina Swoboda, the former Arizona Republican Party chairwoman."},{"title":"Amidst hiring push, police experts question ICE officer vetting : NPR","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/07/23/nx-s1-5903173/police-experts-question-ice-officer-vetting","description":"**Amidst hiring push, police experts question ICE officer vetting** **The recent fatal shootings by ICE officers in Texas and Maine come at a time when the federal agency has hired a huge number of officers quickly. An FBI official places an evidence card in Biddeford where a man was killed in a shooting involving U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement agents on Monday, July 13, 2026. An FBI official places an evidence card in Biddeford where a man was killed in a shooting involving U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement agents on July 13, 2026. The killing, along with a fatal ICE shooting in Texas the week before, comes on the heels of a rapid hiring spree at the federal agency: ICE says it has hired more than 12,000 officers in less than a year. That has prompted some law enforcement experts to worry that ICE is cutting corners in its hiring – a lesson police have learned the hard way over the years."},{"title":"Ukraine’s new military chief pledges to escalate retaliation against Russia","url":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/22/ukraines-new-military-chief-pledges-to-escalate-retaliation-against-russia","description":"Major General Mykhailo Drapatyi attends a meeting with Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelenskiy, amid Russia's attack on Ukraine, in Kyiv, Ukraine July 20, 2026. Ukraine's newly appointed military chief Mykhailo Drapatyi [File: Ukrainian Presidential Press Service/Handout via Reuters]. Mykhailo Drapatyi, named commander of Ukraine’s armed forces on Wednesday, said the military would “continue and intensify offensive operations in all domains” as Kyiv seeks to pressure Russian forces. Russia’s Ministry of Defence claimed a series of attacks on Ukraine Wednesday, including on cargo ships and storage facilities. Russian attacks also hit loading and storage facilities at Odesa, a cargo ship and a bulk carrier that were transporting cargo ⁠to Odesa for the ⁠Ukrainian military, and three warehouses storing drones, according to the ministry. Russia’s Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov said Wednesday he would meet with his US counterpart Marco Rubio on Thursday in Manila on the sidelines of the ASEAN gathering of south-east Asian foreign ministers."},{"title":"Wisconsin police officer shoots, kills man on city street near state Capitol in Madison","url":"https://apnews.com/article/madison-wisconsin-police-shooting-7755ab2ec68bda1a7d7890394f4b1267","description":"Test Your News I.Q. 2026 Elections Election Results Election calendar White House Congress Supreme Court The latest AP-NORC polls. (AP) — A Madison police officer shot and killed a man who had injured him with a knife while resisting arrest, the city’s police chief said Wednesday. A Madison police officer shot and killed a man who had injured him with a knife while resisting arrest, the city’s police chief said Wednesday. The race of the officer who fired his gun on Wednesday and that of the man killed were not released by police."},{"title":"Houthis attack oil tankers in Red Sea as Iran, Trump battle over trade routes","url":"https://www.nbcnews.com/world/iran/houthis-attack-oil-tankers-red-sea-iran-trump-battle-trade-hormuz-rcna588842","description":"Morning Rundown: Tropical Storm Bertha makes landfall, spike in Secret Service threats, and some moviegoers want an AI ‘Odyssey’. IE 11 is not supported. For an optimal experience visit our site on another browser. # Oil tankers attacked as Iran threatens to turn new trade route into a ‘Gate of Tears’. The Iran-backed Houthi militants said Thursday that they had struck two tankers in the Red Sea, risking another bottleneck that could further disrupt global energy supplies, spike prices and heap domestic political pressure on President Donald Trump. Satellite imagery of the Bab el-Mandeb Strait.Gallo Images / Copernicus Sentinel Data 2026 via Getty Images. Add NBC News to Google. Far from easing the crisis over a key global trade route, Iran has now moved to create another critical chokepoint. The Tehran-backed Houthi militants said Thursday that they had struck two tankers in the Red Sea, risking another bottleneck. We are getting your experience ready."},{"title":"DHS chief’s early missteps and an AI-generated version of ‘The Odyssey’: Morning Rundown","url":"https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/markwayne-mullin-missteps-ai-generated-odyssey-morning-rundown-rcna588839","description":"Morning Rundown: Tropical Storm Bertha makes landfall, spike in Secret Service threats, and some moviegoers want an AI ‘Odyssey’. # DHS chief’s early missteps and an AI-generated version of ‘The Odyssey’: Morning Rundown. Plus, the Secret Service said it has opened over 10,000 investigations into threats on its protectees so far this year, a 40% increase from the same time last year. Tension is brewing between the White House and Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin, sources say. The Secret Service is seeing a drastic increase in threats to the people it protects. There appears to be a growing tension between Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin and the White House after some early missteps, according to a senior Trump administration official and a senior Department of Homeland Security official. Mullin, the former Oklahoma senator who is only four months into his role, was brought in to lead a department at the forefront of President Donald Trump’s signature domestic policy — immigration — and as the administration is trying to reboot its mass deportation effort."}],"name":"web_search_tavily","tool_call_id":"toolu_019xz9y8vDETqkHttp2mWvu5"},{"role":"tool","content":[{"title":"Oil Prices Surge After Attacks in Red Sea: Iran War Live ...","url":"https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/07/23/world/iran-war-strikes-oil-trump","description":"A total of 18 U.S. service members have died in the war since it started on Feb. 28, and about 550 have been wounded so far in the conflict. At least 1,700 Iranian civilians have been killed in the war, according to the Human Rights Activists News Agency, a rights group focused on Iran.\n\nIn the days since a fragile truce collapsed this month, the Pentagon has provided little information about the American military strategy after nearly five months of war. The last major Pentagon briefing on the war was in early May.\n\nIn statements after airstrikes against Iranian military sites last week, U.S. Central Command said it was retaliating for Iran’s attacks against commercial ships transiting the Strait of Hormuz, never mentioning Iran’s strikes on bases in the region, including in Jordan. [...] U.S. Central Command, which coordinates military operations in the Middle East, said it had launched another wave of strikes overnight to degrade Iran’s ability to threaten commercial shipping, targeting Iranian drone and missile storage sites and air defenses.\n\nAt the same time, Iran’s Islamic Revolutionary Guards Corps said that it had launched attacks on U.S. military targets in Jordan and Kuwait, including a communications tower, accusing U.S. forces of attacking equivalent infrastructure in Iran.\n\nHours before the latest exchange of fire, Mr. Trump threatened to attack a bridge or power plant “any time the Islamic republic of Iran shoots at a ship in the Strait of Hormuz,” and a week after he said the U.S. would reinstate a naval blockade of Iranian ports. [...] A cease-fire deal agreement reached last month by the two countries has unraveled in recent weeks, with the Strait of Hormuz as the focal point of the dispute. The conflict is evolving into a battle to control waterways in the region, which were fully open to marine traffic before the war and are critical to the flow of global oil and gas supplies.\n\nHere’s what else we’re covering:\n\n The return of dead soldiers: President Trump, before attending the return of four Army soldiers killed in Iranian strikes, told reporters that they had died supportive of the war he was waging. He did not say how he knew their views. Americans, including Republicans, have grown more critical of the war."},{"title":"Trump attends dignified transfer of service members as Iran war escalates","url":"https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/trump-administration/live-blog/trump-dignified-transfer-service-members-iran-congress-election-rcna588638","description":"The United States unleashed its 11th night of strikes as Trump threatened to hit a key underground nuclear facility next, and Iran launched new attacks on U.S. allies across the Middle East today.\n\nDespite renewed diplomatic efforts to revive the deal between Tehran and Washington, the two sides have intensified their clash centered on the Strait of Hormuz. With shipping through that crucial waterway increasingly throttled, Iran has also opened a new front in its campaign to pressure the global economy by disrupting another key trade route: the Red Sea.\n\nAnd the toll is rising — on American and Iranian lives, energy prices, civilian infrastructure across the region and financial costs to the U.S. government.\n\nRead the full story here. [...] The strikes targeted Iranian maritime capabilities, missile and drone storage facilities, coastal surveillance sites and air defense assets, the command said in a post on X, adding that the strikes “further degrade Iran’s ability to attack civilian mariners and commercial vessels.”\n\nThe U.S. resumed strikes on Iran this month as the two countries vie for control of the Strait of Hormuz, a crucial trading route. Iran has responded with drone and missile attacks on U.S. bases and other targets in the region, including an attack in Jordan that killed three U.S. service members over the weekend. [...] Their deaths, along with that of an Army soldier killed over the weekend during a “controlled detonation” of a downed Iranian drone in Iraq, bring the total number of U.S. service member deaths in the Iran war to 18. Thousands of Iranians and others across the Middle East have also been killed since the war began with U.S.-Israeli strikes on Feb. 28.\n\n## Disgraced former Gov. Blagojevich wants Trump to pardon a politician on the lam in Greece\n\nRod Blagojevich stands outside at night\n\nFormer Illinois Gov. Rod Blagojevich at a news conference outside his home in Chicago after President Donald Trump pardoned him in February 2025. Scott Olson / Getty Images file"},{"title":"Iran war live: US launches new attacks; Houthis ...","url":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/7/23/iran-war-live-us-launches-new-attacks-houthis-attack-2-saudi-oil-tankers","description":"Skip linksSkip to Content\n\nLive\n\nLive\n\nLive updatesLive updates,\n\n# Iran war live: US launches new attacks; Houthis attack 2 Saudi oil tankers\n\nUS and Iran trade new threats as more explosions are reported in Iranian cities, including Sirik, Ahvaz and Bushehr.\n\nVessels at the Strait of Hormuz, as seen from Musandam, Oman, July 21, 2026. REUTERS/Stringer\n\nVideo Duration 11 minutes 54 seconds 11:54\n\n'No better way' to end US-Iran war than diplomacy: Martin Griffiths\n\n\n\nBy Lyndal Rowlands, Zaid Sabah and Adam Hancock\n\nPublished On 23 Jul 202623 Jul 2026\n\nSave\n\nShare [...] Published On 23 Jul 202623 Jul 2026\n\nSave\n\nShare\n\n The US bombs Iran for the 12th consecutive night as Iranian media report explosions in the cities of Bushehr, Ahvaz and Sirik.\n Iran’s Minister of Foreign Affairs, Abbas Araghchi, says Tehran’s defence doctrine is an “eye for an eye”, after US President Donald Trump warned that American forces would bomb an Iranian bridge or power plant every time Iranian forces hit a ship in the Strait of Hormuz.\n\n---"},{"title":"Iran War Updates: Trump threatens to bomb a bridge or ...","url":"https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/iran-war-us-trump-strait-hormuz-oil-prices-red-sea-houthis","description":"Watch CBS News\n\n \n\n# Iran War Updates: Oil back near $95 a barrel as U.S. insists Strait of Hormuz is open despite attacks on ships\n\nBy\n\nFrank Andrews\n\nFrank Andrews is a CBS News journalist based in London.\n\nRead Full Bio\n\nFrank Andrews,\n\nTucker Reals\n\nTucker Reals is CBSNews.com's foreign editor, based in the CBS News London bureau. He has worked for CBS News since 2006, prior to which he worked for The Associated Press in Washington, D.C., and London.\n\nRead Full Bio\n\nTucker Reals\n\n/ CBS News\n\nAdd CBS News on Google\n\n## What to know about the Iran war today: [...] The two missiles that weren't intercepted \"fell in two uninhabited areas free of population, without any injuries or material damage occurring,\" the bulletin read.\n\nIran's semi-official Fars News agency said the Iranian military had targeted a storage depot at the Al-Azraq Air Base in Jordan with drones early Wednesday morning local time.\n\nUntil recently, Jordan had largely avoided Iranian attacks, despite hosting U.S. troops. But Iran has ramped up its bombardment of the kingdom in recent days, killing three U.S. soldiers at an air base over the weekend.\n\nBy Frank Andrews \n\n link copied\n\n## Trump to attend dignified transfer for deceased U.S. service members Wednesday [...] Pvt. Isabella Gonzales, 19, of Carrollton, Texas, and 1st Lt. Tyler James Feehan, 25, of Ewa Beach, Hawaii, were also killed in the attack on the Muwaffaq Salti Air Base over the weekend.\n\n 3soldiers.jpg  \n\nFrom left: Pvt. Isabella Gonzales, 19, of Carrollton, Texas; Sgt. Angel Rampersad, 28, of Ozone Park, New York; and 1st Lt. Tyler James Feehan, 25, of Ewa Beach, Hawaii, were killed in an Iranian attack at a base in Jordan.  Department of Defense\n\n \n\nEighteen American service members are believed to have been killed since the start of the Iran war, including four U.S. soldiers in recent days — three in an Iranian attack on Jordan and one during a controlled demolition of an Iranian drone in Iraq.\n\nBy Joe Walsh \n\n link copied"},{"title":"Tehran responds after Trump warns US will strike bridges ...","url":"https://www.foxnews.com/live-news/iran-war-trump-strait-of-hormuz-oil-07-22-26","description":"Elements of the Trump administration are again debating whether the United States should support the downfall of Iran’s government as the war intensifies again, according to a recent analysis.\n\nThe analysis, published Monday by Financial Times columnist Gideon Rachman, says the discussion reportedly includes efforts to encourage an internal uprising and the possible revival of proposals to support armed Iranian opposition groups, including Kurdish factions that have fought Iran for decades.\n\nThe question has gained urgency as the United States resumes strikes against Iranian military targets and officials debate whether continued pressure should remain narrowly focused on maritime security or evolve into a broader effort to weaken the Islamic Republic from within."},{"title":"Houthis claim Red Sea attacks, threatening new chokepoint in conflict","url":"https://www.cnn.com/2026/07/23/world/live-news/iran-war-trump","description":"Meanwhile, the US military said it launcheda new wave of strikes against Iran, and Iran’s revolutionary guards said Thursday local time that an oil tanker caught fire following an explosion near the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nHere’s what else to know:\n\nEBOF Liptak Digi Still.jpg\nEBOF Liptak Digi Still.jpg\n\nCNN’s Aleena Fayaz, Alejandra Jaramillo, Elise Hammond, Lex Harvey, Tal Shalev, Sophie Tanno and August Phillips contributed reporting to this post.\n\n## A new front is opening in the Iran war. Oil faces \"no way out\"\n\nDavid Goldman\nSatellite view of the Bab al-Mandeb Strait.\n\nThe Houthis’ blockade of the Bab al-Mandeb strait couldn’t have come at a worse time for the oil market. [...] “If the Americans target an Iranian bridge or power plant, Iran will respond by striking infrastructure and bridges across the region, including energy facilities in which the United States has interests,” the unnamed official was reported as saying by the Iranian semi-official news agency, Tasnim.\n\nTehran also reiterated that it will “exercise its sovereignty over the Strait of Hormuz,” the Iranian military official said.\n\n## Houthis claim Red Sea attacks as US conducts more Iran strikes. Catch up here\n\nYemen’s Iran-backed Houthis claimed to have struck two Saudi oil tankers in the Red Sea early on Thursday morning local time, an attack that threatens to create a new front in the war with Iran. [...] Iran’s proxies in Yemen have threatened to cut off a major bypass of the Strait of Hormuz, through which oil tanker traffic has slowed dramatically in recent days.\n\nAn effective Houthi blockade of Bab al-Mandeb would create a new front in the war with Iran, potentially requiring US military intervention that could then erode US ability to help ships out of the Strait of Hormuz. It also could prevent the Saudis from getting diesel to Europe at a crucial time for the fuel market.