 a tour of `lib/X/`\" / \"What's in this module?\"\n- \"How does X work across the codebase?\"\n- \"Walk me through the compilation pipeline\"\n- Anything where a good answer requires reading 5+ files and\n  organizing the findings by theme.\n\nDo NOT use the explorer for:\n- Specific factual lookups (\"what does function X return?\") — use\n  `codeAgent`.\n- Plan critique or bug diagnosis — use `oracleAgent`.\n- External / web research — use `researchAgent`.\n\n**Tell the user when you're consulting the explorer.** \"Let me have\nthe explorer go through the docs and put together an overview.\" Like\nwith the oracle, the visibility is part of the value — the user\nbenefits from knowing breadth is being applied.\n\nPass the explorer a self-contained question with explicit scope\n(\"all of `docs/site/guide/`\", \"the `lib/parsers/` module\"). It does\nnot see your conversation. Be clear about the level of detail\nexpected.\n\n## Style\n\nNever start a response by calling the user's question or idea good,\ngreat, fascinating, profound, excellent, or perfect. Skip flattery\nand respond directly. Don't pad replies with \"happy to help\",\n\"certainly\", or trailing summaries the user can read in the diff.\n\n**Use ASCII diagrams when they clarify.** For control flow, state\nmachines, pipelines, module relationships, or any \"how do the parts\nfit together\" answer, draw a small ASCII diagram in a fenced\n```text block. Boxes, arrows, and trees beat paragraphs for\nstructural explanations:\n\n```text\nparse → SymbolTable.build → preprocess → TypeScriptBuilder → printTs\n```\n\nKeep diagrams small. Skip them where prose or code is clearer —\ndiagrams earn their space by showing **relationships** or **flow**.\n\n## Be proactive\n\nWhen the user asks you to look at, debug, or change a file or some code,\n**delegate to `codeAgent` to do it** — don't ask the user to paste a file\nor describe code a subagent could read. The code agent has `read`, `glob`,\nand `ls` and resolves relative paths against the user's working directory\nautomatically, so a bare filename like `foo.agency` is enough. Only ask\nthe user for information you genuinely cannot obtain through a subagent.\n\n## Answer before action\n\nWhen the user asks a question, asks for an opinion, or asks how to\nplan or approach something, **answer the question first**. Don't\njump straight into delegating to a subagent or making tool calls\nunless the user has clearly asked for an action (\"do X\", \"fix Y\",\n\"build Z\"). If the user is exploring or thinking out loud, think\nwith them — don't sprint to implementation.\n\n## Communicating with the user\n- Make sure the user is following what you're doing. Use the `whatIAmDoing` tool frequently to tell the user what you're doing.\n- Also use the `elapsedTime` tool frequently to check how much time has elapsed since you started the task. If the user gave you a time constraint to work within, make sure you finish the task within that time constraint. For simple tasks, make sure you don't spend too long researching things before giving an answer.\n\n\n  Current date: 2026-07-23\n  Current working directory: /Users/adityabhargava/agency-lang/packages/agency-lang\n  \n  "},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What are today's top news stories? Please answer quickly"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"toolCalls":[{"id":"toolu_01Y9NDSYrTwQsxNNRSeX351g","name":"whatIAmDoing","arguments":{"message":"Looking up today's top news headlines via web search."}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"Delivered message to user.","name":"whatIAmDoing","tool_call_id":"toolu_01Y9NDSYrTwQsxNNRSeX351g"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"toolCalls":[{"id":"toolu_014ezrqd7jgrLt3gimVnWHCB","name":"researchAgent","arguments":{"userMsg":"What are the top news stories today, July 23, 2026? Please give a brief summary of the 5-6 most significant world/national news headlines from today.","agencyTopic":false}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"## Top News — July 23, 2026\n\n**US-Iran War Escalates, New Front Opens in Red Sea**\n\nThe conflict has reached its 12th consecutive night of US strikes, with Iranian media reporting explosions in the cities of Bushehr, Ahvaz and Sirik. Iran's Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said Tehran's defence doctrine is an \"eye for an eye,\" after Trump warned US forces would bomb an Iranian bridge or power plant every time Iranian forces hit a ship in the Strait of Hormuz. The US has also deployed heavier firepower — the US military used a B-1 long-range bomber on Tuesday to strike Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps targets in Iran, the first such mission since fighting resumed 12 days ago. Meanwhile, Iran-backed Houthi militants said they struck two tankers in the Red Sea, risking a new chokepoint that could further disrupt global energy supplies and spike prices. The human toll continues to climb: a total of 18 US service members have died in the war, and about 550 have been wounded so far, while at least 1,700 Iranian civilians have been killed.\n\n**Ukraine's Military Shakeup Concludes After Week of Protests**\n\nAfter a week of nationwide protests over the direction of Ukraine's military strategy, the Ukrainian military has a new commander, Mykhailo Drapatyi, replacing Gen. Oleksandr Syrsky following the controversial firing of popular defense minister Mykhailo Fedorov. The new chief is not backing down from the fight — Drapatyi said the military would \"continue and intensify offensive operations in all domains\" as Kyiv seeks to pressure Russian forces, while Russia claimed fresh strikes on Odesa port facilities and drone warehouses.\n\n**World Cup Final Ends in Drama and Controversy**\n\nSpain beat Argentina 1-0 in extra time to win its first World Cup title since 2010, with Ferran Torres scoring in the 106th minute, denying Lionel Messi one more shot at glory. The match wasn't without controversy — a post-match brawl broke out involving Argentina's Leandro Paredes and Spain's Eric García, prompting FIFA to launch an investigation.\n\n**Trump's Election-Fraud Claims Draw Scrutiny**\n\nPresident Trump has renewed his push on election integrity: in a primetime address, Trump said the People's Republic of China carried out what is believed to be the largest compromise of election data in history, resulting in China's acquisition of 220 million US voter files, and touted a DHS report claiming roughly 278,000 noncitizens are registered to vote in federal elections. However, newly declassified documents cited by the administration to back these claims offer scant evidence, and don't fully support Trump's allegations. Separately, Secretary of State Marco Rubio struck a cordial tone with China's top diplomat despite Trump's meddling claims, in an apparent bid to ease relations ahead of Xi Jinping's expected US visit in September.\n\n**China Landslide Recovery Continues in Chongqing**\n\nRescue operations remain underway after a landslide in southwestern China's Chongqing killed at least eight people and left 34 missing, after massive amounts of rock and soil buried more than 10 residential buildings in Pengshui County amid heavy rain.\n\n**Domestic Notes: ICE Vetting Concerns & Wisconsin Police Shooting**\n\nRecent fatal shootings by ICE officers in Texas and Maine have raised questions from police experts about vetting, as the agency has hired more than 12,000 officers in less than a year amid its rapid hiring push. Separately, a Madison, Wisconsin police officer shot and killed a man who had injured him with a knife while resisting arrest near the state Capitol.\n\n---\n*Sources: NYT, NBC News, Al Jazeera, CBS News, Axios, Washington Post, AP, CNN.*","name":"researchAgent","tool_call_id":"toolu_014ezrqd7jgrLt3gimVnWHCB"},{"role":"assistant","content":"Here's a quick rundown of today's top stories (July 23, 2026):\n\n1. **US-Iran War Escalates** — 12th straight night of US strikes on Iran; a B-1 bomber hit IRGC targets, Houthi militants struck two tankers in the Red Sea, opening a new front. Toll so far: 18 US troops killed, ~550 wounded, ~1,700 Iranian civilians dead.\n\n2. **Ukraine Military Shakeup** — After a week of protests, Mykhailo Drapatyi takes over as military chief from Gen. Syrsky. He's vowing to intensify offensive operations as Russia strikes Odesa's port.\n\n3. **World Cup Final Drama** — Spain beat Argentina 1-0 in extra time (Ferran Torres, 106') for their first title since 2010, denying Messi one last shot. A post-match brawl has triggered a FIFA investigation.\n\n4. **Trump's Election-Fraud Claims** — Trump alleges China stole 220 million US voter files and that ~278,000 noncitizens are registered to vote; declassified docs cited don't fully back this up. Meanwhile Rubio is trying to smooth relations with Beijing ahead of a possible Xi visit in September.\n\n5. **China Landslide** — In Chongqing's Pengshui County, a landslide killed at least 8 and left 34 missing after burying over 10 buildings.\n\n6. **Domestic Notes** — Scrutiny over ICE officer vetting after fatal shootings in Texas and Maine amid rapid hiring (12,000+ new officers); a Madison, WI police officer fatally shot a man who had stabbed him near the state Capitol.\n\nLet me know if you'd like more depth on any of these."}],"parentId":null,"hidden":false,"label":"main","summary":null}},"counter":3,"activeStack":["0"],"sessions":{"budgetParse":"1","main":"2"}}},"timeTaken":362060.52508399996,"timestamp":"2026-07-23T14:00:43.498Z"}}
