LM calls in Agency, including retries, timeouts, backoff, and other options.</description>\n    <location>llm-part-2.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>LLM Calls</name>\n    <description>How to make LLM calls in Agency, including structured outputs via type annotations, model and provider configuration, streaming, tool use, and memory.</description>\n    <location>llm.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>MCP Servers</name>\n    <description>How to connect your Agency agents to external Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers via the `@agency-lang/mcp` package to use their tools.</description>\n    <location>mcp.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Memory</name>\n    <description>How to give an agent long-term memory — remembering facts across runs, recalling them on demand, auto-injecting them into LLM calls, and approving what gets written.</description>\n    <location>memory.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Message threads</name>\n    <description>Explains how Agency&apos;s default shared message history works across LLM calls, and how `thread` and subthread blocks let you create isolated conversations that don&apos;t pollute the main history.</description>\n    <location>message-threads.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Nodes</name>\n    <description>Describes nodes — Agency&apos;s entry points into agents — and how they differ from functions, including the permanent transition behavior and the special role of the `main` node.</description>\n    <location>nodes.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Notes on Types</name>\n    <description>Notes on advanced type usage in Agency, including the intentionally-unsound covariance of `Record&lt;K, V&gt;` in both type parameters and the tradeoffs that motivated the choice.</description>\n    <location>notes-on-types.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Observability</name>\n    <description>How to enable Agency&apos;s structured logging for 