ween Agency and TypeScript — importing TS functions and modules into Agency code, and importing compiled Agency nodes into TypeScript, along with the limitations of each direction.</description>\n    <location>ts-interop.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Type Validation</name>\n    <description>Describes the `@validate` and related annotations that let you attach arbitrary validation logic to types, used by the bang (`!`) operator and other runtime validators.</description>\n    <location>type-validation.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Types</name>\n    <description>Reference for Agency&apos;s TypeScript-like type system — including primitives, unions, arrays, objects, and built-in generics — and how types automatically generate Zod schemas for structured LLM outputs.</description>\n    <location>types.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>unused.md</name>\n    <description></description>\n    <location>unused.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>unused2.md</name>\n    <description></description>\n    <location>unused2.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Value-Parameterized Types</name>\n    <description>Describes how to use value-parameterized type aliases in Agency, allowing for compile-time substitution of values in type annotations and validation.</description>\n    <location>value-parameterized-types.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>VSCode Extension</name>\n    <description>Install instructions for the Agency VS Code extension and an overview of the language server (LSP) that powers editor features like typechecking.</description>\n    <location>vscode-extension.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Why Agency?</name>\n    <description>Explains why Agency was created, and what makes it different from other programming languages like TypeScript or Python.</description>\n    <location>why-agency.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>","schema":{"def":{"type":"object","