tionally omits.</description>\n    <location>agency-vs-typescript.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Attachments</name>\n    <description>Explains how to send images and files to the model in an LLM call, using the `image()` and `file()` builders from `std::thread`.</description>\n    <location>attachments.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Basic syntax</name>\n    <description>Overview of Agency&apos;s TypeScript-derived syntax, covering primitive types, variables, arrays, objects, functions, and other core language constructs.</description>\n    <location>basic-syntax.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Blocks</name>\n    <description>Explains Agency&apos;s block syntax, used in place of lambdas for higher-order functions like `map`, including custom block-taking functions and inline block syntax.</description>\n    <location>blocks.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Build Integration</name>\n    <description>How to compile Agency code as part of a JavaScript build (Vite, etc.) and call it from the backend of a web app.</description>\n    <location>build-integration.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Built-in Functions</name>\n    <description>Reference table of every user-facing built-in function and language construct available in any `.agency` file without an import, covering LLM calls, checkpointing, interrupts, concurrency, and more.</description>\n    <location>builtins.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Callbacks</name>\n    <description>Documents Agency&apos;s callback hooks (e.g. `onNodeStart`) that can be registered from Agency files via `callback(...)` or passed in from TypeScript when invoking a node, including scoping rules and the no-interrupts-in-callbacks restriction.</description>\n    <location>callbacks.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Checkpointing</name>\n    <description>Explains Agency&apos;s ability to snapshot and restore execution state via `checkpoint`, `getCheckpoint`, and `restore`, which underpins interrupts, error recovery, forks, and the time-travel debugger.</description>\n    <location>checkpointing.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Common functions</name>\n    <description>How to use common functions in Agency, such as printing, getting user input, and reading/writing files.</description>\n    <location>common-functions.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Compiling and Running Code</name>\n    <description>How to run Agency programs and compile them to JavaScript, including watch mode and the classic global-install gotcha.</description>\n    <location>compiling-and-running.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>List comprehensions</name>\n    <description>Build a new list from an existing one in a single expression, either sequentially or concurrently.</description>\n    <location>comprehensions.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Concurrency</name>\n    <description>Covers Agency&apos;s concurrency primitives including `parallel`, `seq`, `fork`, and `race` for running multiple branches of work simultaneously.</description>\n    <location>concurrency.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Cross-thread context sharing</name>\n    <description>How to inspect, read, and resume other `thread {}` blocks in the same run using `listThreads()`, `getThread()`, and the named arguments on `thread {}`. Includes a categorize-and-route example.</description>\n    <location>cross-thread-context.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Custom Providers</name>\n    <description>How to create and use custom model providers in Agency.</description>\n    <location>custom-providers.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Debugging</name>\n    <description>A walkthrough of Agency&apos;s interactive terminal debugger — stepping forward, rewinding through checkpoints, overriding variables, and inspecting panels.</description>\n    <location>debugging.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Developer Tools</name>\n    <description>A quick tour of the typecheck, doc, and format commands — static tools that check, document, and tidy your Agency code without running it.</description>\n    <location>developer-tools.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Effect Sets and `raises`</name>\n    <description>Declare the interrupt effects a function may raise with a `raises` clause, group them into reusable `effectSet`s, and raise interrupts as statements with `raise` — all checked at compile time.</description>\n    <location>effects-and-raises.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Effects</name>\n    <description>Explains how to use effects in interrupts, allowing handlers to respond differently based on the effect.</description>\n    <location>effects.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Error handling</name>\n    <description>Describes Agency&apos;s exception-free approach to errors using the `Result` type, with `success`/`failure` constructors and patterns for unwrapping results.</description>\n    <location>error-handling.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Exercise 1</name>\n    <description>Practice exercises for getting familiar with Agency&apos;s syntax and features.</description>\n    <location>exercise-1.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Functions</name>\n    <description>Covers function declarations in Agency, including docstrings (used as LLM tool descriptions), default and variadic arguments, named parameters, and block syntax.</description>\n    <location>functions.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Getting Started</name>\n    <description>Introduction to the Agency language guide, covering installation, a quick &quot;hello world&quot; example, and how to navigate the rest of the documentation.</description>\n    <location>getting-started.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Global variable initialization</name>\n    <description>How Agency initializes top-level values that reference other top-level values from different modules.</description>\n    <location>global-var-initialization.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Global vs Static Variables</name>\n    <description>Per-run isolation of global variables vs static variables that initialize once and persist across runs.</description>\n    <location>global-vs-static.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Guards</name>\n    <description>Documents the `guard` function for capping the cost and/or compute time of a block of work, returning a `Result` that lets you handle budget overruns.</description>\n    <location>guards.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Handlers</name>\n    <description>Explains how `handle ... with` blocks let agents respond to interrupts — approving, rejecting, or propagating them.</description>\n    <location>handlers.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Image Generation</name>\n    <description>Generate images from a prompt with generateImage(), persist them with writeBinary(), and feed them back into multimodal llm() calls.</description>\n    <location>image-generation.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Imports and packages</name>\n    <description>Describes how Agency&apos;s JavaScript-style import syntax works for relative paths, npm packages, the `std::` standard library, and the `pkg::` prefix for npm-distributed Agency packages.</description>\n    <location>imports-and-packages.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Interrupts from TypeScript</name>\n    <description>How to run an Agency node from TypeScript, receive the interrupts it raises, and respond to them to resume execution — the building block for approval flows and human-in-the-loop apps.</description>\n    <location>interrupts-from-typescript.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Interrupts, Part 2</name>\n    <description>Continues the discussion on Agency&apos;s interrupt feature, focusing on advanced usage and scenarios.</description>\n    <location>interrupts-part-2.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Interrupts</name>\n    <description>Introduces Agency&apos;s interrupt feature for pausing execution and asking for user approval.</description>\n    <location>interrupts.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>LLM Calls, Part 2</name>\n    <description>More about LLM calls in Agency, including retries, timeouts, backoff, and other options.</description>\n    <location>llm-part-2.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>LLM Calls</name>\n    <description>How to make LLM calls in Agency, including structured outputs via type annotations, model and provider configuration, streaming, tool use, and memory.</description>\n    <location>llm.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>MCP Servers</name>\n    <description>How to connect your Agency agents to external Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers via the `@agency-lang/mcp` package to use their tools.</description>\n    <location>mcp.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Memory</name>\n    <description>How to give an agent long-term memory — remembering facts across runs, recalling them on demand, auto-injecting them into LLM calls, and approving what gets written.</description>\n    <location>memory.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Message threads</name>\n    <description>Explains how Agency&apos;s default shared message history works across LLM calls, and how `thread` and subthread blocks let you create isolated conversations that don&apos;t pollute the main history.</description>\n    <location>message-threads.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Nodes</name>\n    <description>Describes nodes — Agency&apos;s entry points into agents — and how they differ from functions, including the permanent transition behavior and the special role of the `main` node.</description>\n    <location>nodes.md</location>\n  </skill>\n