a-parameter-injection.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Schemas</name>\n    <description>Shows how Agency converts types into runtime Zod schemas via the `schema()` function and the bang (`!`) shorthand for validating values and function parameters.</description>\n    <location>schemas.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Serving Agency Code</name>\n    <description>How to expose exported Agency functions and nodes over MCP (stdio) or HTTP REST so external tools and services can invoke your agents.</description>\n    <location>serving.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>State Isolation</name>\n    <description>Explains how every Agency node call gets isolated state when invoked from TypeScript, and how `static` variables let you share expensive one-time initialization across runs.</description>\n    <location>state-isolation.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Streaming</name>\n    <description>Explains how to stream responses from the model in an LLM call, using the `stream` option and `onStream` callback.</description>\n    <location>streaming.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Tags and Redaction</name>\n    <description>Attach arbitrary tags to values with std::tag, and use the built-in redact tag to keep secrets like API keys out of state logs.</description>\n    <location>tags.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Testing</name>\n    <description>Introduces Agency&apos;s built-in testing framework, covering fixture generation, exact-match versus LLM-as-judge fixtures, and running tests with the `test` command.</description>\n    <location>testing.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>Troubleshooting</name>\n    <description>Solutions to common issues when using Agency, such as module-not-found errors from global installs and other gotchas.</description>\n    <location>troubleshooting.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>TypeScript helpers — the `agency.*` namespace</nam